WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:10.879
ok so for this problem we've got a
substrate here which has a ketone

00:00:10.879 --> 00:00:15.089
functional group and a carboxylic acid
functional group and we're gonna do two

00:00:15.089 --> 00:00:19.200
reactions with two different reducing
agents, we've got sodium borohydride and

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:22.700
we've got lithium aluminum hydride so if
you don't remember what these do go ahead and

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:26.140
check out my lecture on oxidation
reduction in the organic chemistry

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:30.970
playlist and we we these are going to
generate two different products based on

00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:35.640
reducing capacity of these two reducing
agents so go and see if you can draw the

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:38.040
correct product for these two
transformations

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:57.940
we've got our two different reducing agents, our sodium
borohydride and a lithium

00:00:57.950 --> 00:01:03.410
aluminum hydride, we know that those are
both sources of hydride, H- which is

00:01:03.410 --> 00:01:07.619
going to act as a nucleophile and
attack a carbonyl and reduce some carbonyl

00:01:07.619 --> 00:01:10.909
containing compound but the key
difference here is that sodium

00:01:10.909 --> 00:01:15.429
borohydride is a softer reducing agent, a
bit weaker whereas lithium aluminum

00:01:15.429 --> 00:01:18.979
hydride is a hard reducing agent, it's a little
bit stronger, so there is a

00:01:18.979 --> 00:01:23.229
discrepancy in what kind of carbonyl
containing compounds they are able to

00:01:23.229 --> 00:01:28.380
reduce. as it happens sodium borohydride is
only able to reduce ketones and

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:34.409
aldehydes, this is because the ketone
an aldehyde functional group, the partial

00:01:34.409 --> 00:01:38.869
positivity on this carbonyl carbon
differs from that of say a carboxylic

00:01:38.869 --> 00:01:44.409
acid because this carbon there, remember
that this adjacent oxygen atom is able to

00:01:44.409 --> 00:01:49.590
put electrons onto that carbon via
resonance and so that is the reason that

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:55.020
this is not as partially positive and
therefore not as electrophilic as a ketone

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:59.329
or aldehyde carbon so that means that
just the discrepancy in electrophilicity

00:01:59.329 --> 00:02:04.069
between this and this means that sodium
borohydride is able to reduce this

00:02:04.069 --> 00:02:08.909
ketone functional group but not the
carboxylic acid functional group so as a

00:02:08.909 --> 00:02:10.450
result on the product

00:02:10.450 --> 00:02:14.590
reducing with sodium borohydride we can
see that the ketone has been reduced to

00:02:14.590 --> 00:02:18.830
the secondary alcohol whereas the
carboxylic acid has been untouched

00:02:18.830 --> 00:02:22.780
it does not have the ability to reduce the
carboxylic acid so that won't change

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:28.340
however lithium aluminum hydride is able
to reduce ketones, aldehydes, carboxylic

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:33.360
acids, esters, it is a stronger reducing
agent because it's stronger it is

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:38.410
able to reduce not just this but also
this and so as a result we can see in

00:02:38.410 --> 00:02:42.849
the product that the ketone has been
reduced to the secondary alcohol just as

00:02:42.849 --> 00:02:47.260
it has over here but in addition the
carboxylic acid has been reduced to the

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:51.280
primary alcohol. so the key here is
noticing the difference in reducing

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:56.500
strength of these two reducing agents and
using that information to draw the to

00:02:56.500 --> 00:02:57.360
correct products

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:22.580
thanks for watching guys, subscribe to my
channel for more tutorials and as always

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:23.750
feel free to email me

